---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'

import installTranscript from '@/assets/projects/canon.png'
import RouteFoot from '@/components/site/case-study/route-foot.astro'
import RouteBar from '@/components/site/nav/route-bar.astro'
import SectionNav from '@/components/site/section-nav/section-nav.astro'
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/base.astro'
import { getLinkTargetAttributes } from '@/lib/links'

const artifactLinks = [
  { label: 'GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/erclx/canon' },
  { label: 'npm', href: 'https://www.npmjs.com/package/@erclx/canon' },
  { label: 'Live build', href: 'https://canon.erclx.dev' },
]

// The route's opening leads the rail, per the note in `jobtriage.astro`.
const navItems = [
  { id: 'canon', label: 'canon' },
  { id: 'problem', label: 'problem' },
  { id: 'standards', label: 'standards' },
  { id: 'workflow', label: 'workflow' },
  { id: 'parallel', label: 'parallel' },
  { id: 'agents', label: 'agents' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="canon · Eric Le"
  description="One set of agent rules, skills, and standards installed into every project and kept in step. The install taxonomy, the adoption thesis, and the operating model behind it."
  surface="case-study"
>
  <SectionNav items={navItems} instant />

  <RouteBar here="canon" />

  <main>
    <section id="canon" class="page-inset pt-16 pb-12 md:pt-20 md:pb-16">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-label tracking-wide uppercase">
          Project
        </p>
        <h1
          data-fade          class="font-display text-foreground mt-4 text-[2.25rem] leading-tight font-semibold md:text-[2.75rem]"
        >
          canon
        </h1>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-lede mt-4"
        >
          A CLI and Claude Code plugin that distributes AI-development rules,
          skills, and workflows from one source, carries the plan, review, and
          ship loop as skills, and coordinates parallel work across separate
          branches.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-3"
        >
          Public and MIT licensed. Installs from npm as
          <code class="font-mono">@erclx/canon</code> and as a Claude Code
          plugin.
        </p>
        <ul
          data-fade          class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6"
        >
          {
            artifactLinks.map((link) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={link.href}
                  {...getLinkTargetAttributes(link.href)}
                  class="text-foreground text-label inline-flex min-h-11 min-w-11 items-center underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
                >
                  {link.label}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        {/* The still closes the opening rather than interrupting it, so a
            reader meets the artifact existing before the argument starts. */}
        <figure
          data-fade          class="figure-breakout bg-card border-border/60 mt-12 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border"
        >
          <Image
            src={installTranscript}
            alt="A terminal installing canon as a Claude Code plugin in three commands, ending with the plugin listed as enabled"
            class="block h-auto w-full"
            loading="eager"
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="problem" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          problem
        </h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          Every project I started got the same agent scaffolding typed into it again, and every copy moved away from the last. I wanted one source I could install rather than a folder I kept re-authoring.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          Drift on this surface is quiet. A rule that fell behind still loads, still reads as authoritative, and produces work against a convention I had already abandoned somewhere else. Nothing fails, so nothing reports it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="standards" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          standards
        </h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          Content reaches a project through three separate operations. <code class="font-mono">install</code> overwrites and runs once to bootstrap. <code class="font-mono">sync</code> updates only files already present and is safe to re-run on a cadence. <code class="font-mono">build</code> derives a payload from current state for pasting into any AI chat.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          Configs overwrite, seeds merge additively and survive local edits, and rules carry a path glob so a Python rule stays dormant in a TypeScript project. That is what makes an update safe to run without reading it first: a sync cannot introduce a surface the project never asked for, and a seed cannot discard the edits made to it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="workflow" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          workflow
        </h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          The rules are not the whole product. The plan, review, and ship loop ships alongside them as skills a session invokes rather than steps a person remembers.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          Review travels on the pull request itself. One skill posts findings to it, another reads them back, fixes each, and pushes a follow-up, and the review then runs again against only the commits that follow-up added.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          The workflow it ships is the workflow that built it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="parallel" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          parallel
        </h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          Work runs across parallel Claude Code sessions split by vantage rather than by capability. One warm orchestrator session holds the cross-feature picture, plans features, and reviews pull requests. It edits no tracked file and it merges nothing. Cold worker sessions each take one plan in their own git worktree, implement it, self-check, and stop at the pull request. The human launches workers and owns the merge.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          The two review passes differ by vantage rather than by depth. A worker's self-review asks whether it built the plan and whether it passes, and it is blind to its own misreadings, because the same misreading wrote both the code and the review. The orchestrator's review is a fresh session carrying the roadmap and every sibling branch in flight, so it can question the plan itself.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          What binds the number of tracks is collision between file sets rather than a fixed cap. A candidate opens only where its files are disjoint from every track already running, and what thins as tracks multiply is the review attention each one gets.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          The claim stops there deliberately, with the token budget named out loud as the ceiling. A larger claim about autonomous fleets is one question about agent count away from falling over.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="agents" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          agents
        </h2>
        <p data-fade class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6">
          All of it depends on agents being able to drive the same tooling without a person reading terminal output beside them.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          Every command has a non-interactive path, every listing emits JSON on stdout, and the framed terminal UI goes to stderr. A skill shelling out to the CLI can treat stdout as data without parsing around a progress frame.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          Verification runs on sandbox scenarios rather than unit tests. Each scenario provisions an isolated project state, asserts which files the run was allowed to touch and whether it escaped that scope, and a disposable GitHub remote lets the skills that depend on <code class="font-mono">gh</code> run end to end, including opening a pull request, merging it, and editing its body.
        </p>
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6">
          That is the part worth stealing. Testing a workflow that opens pull requests means letting it open real ones somewhere it cannot do damage.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  <RouteFoot />

  <script>
    import { initWayHome } from '@/components/site/case-study/way-home'
    import { initReveal } from '@/lib/reveal'

    initReveal()
    initWayHome()
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
